WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.290
What am I worth?

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.680
Well, I've got a few assets

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.480
and to be honest, more than a few debts.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.870
Most people say that to
calculate your net worth,

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.510
you should add up those assets

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.510
and then subtract those debts.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.860
But that really doesn't
show the whole picture.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.490
The thought of having
a net worth above zero

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.460
when you're just starting out,

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.564
maybe you've got student
loans and very little income

00:00:28.564 --> 00:00:30.653
might sound ridiculous and I understand

00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:33.083
because I'm very young and cool too.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.020
But even if you don't have
a lot of money to invest

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.370
you can still invest in...

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.170
Wait, is it really that corny?

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.003
Yeah.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.710
You can still invest in yourself

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.390
or to put it better, you can
invest in your human capital.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.490
With Suzanne Woolley of Bloomberg Wealth.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:56.530
Thank you so much for being on Good Money.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.610
Can you explain to me
what human capital is.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.330
Human capital is basically our potential.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.130
You may feel first starting out

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.820
that you don't have a lot of money,

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.670
maybe you have student debt.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.320
You feel like you're
not very rich in assets.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.590
But the fact is that here's
another corny line for you,

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.940
like you are your biggest asset.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.150
You have years of earning potential.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.710
So you may not have a
lot of money in the bank

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.010
but your potential at that point is huge.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.210
So you're actually rich in that respect

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:30.010
even though you may feel
sort of everyday poor.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.610
Can you explain to me the ways

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.150
in which human and financial
capital sort of converge

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.440
as our lifetime goes by?

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.840
So when you just start out,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.210
you have untapped human capital.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.530
You have huge earnings potential
for decades and decades,

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.640
but you don't have a lot
of financial capital.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.490
Going through life you are
building your financial capital.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.656
You're also building your human capital.

00:01:51.656 --> 00:01:55.710
But say at age 55 or so,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.060
you may be in your peak earning years.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.240
So when you look out down the
road, you know, 20, 30 years,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.030
your earning potential is not that huge.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.650
But by this point you probably have a lot

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.180
of financial capital.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.540
You've saved in your
retirement savings program.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.130
Maybe you have other outside savings.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.610
So at this point your financial capital

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:19.283
might be a little bit ahead
of your human capital, say.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.743
And that's pretty much
sort of as it should be.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.123
So how can I invest in my human capital?

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.073
What actions can I take
to make the most impact?

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.760
Your ability to earn an income

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.890
is one of your greatest assets.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.593
Oh!Hi. Who are you?

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:47.710
I'm Mary Beth Storjohann.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.010
I'm a certified financial planner.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.400
Oh, well, thanks for coming on the show.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.930
How is my career one of my biggest assets?

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.410
That salary that you're bringing in,

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.550
the employer benefits, there is so much

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.020
that can add to your net worth over time

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.470
which is why you need to
really nurture your career

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.550
to make sure you're extracting
everything you can out of it.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.387
What are some steps I can
take to nurture my career?

00:03:09.387 --> 00:03:11.370
The basic ones that we all know about

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.920
are those promotions,
the salary increases,

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.040
and, you know asking for
increased responsibility

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.260
at your job.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.840
The big thing that was misconstrued

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.010
especially for young people

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.600
is those only come around so often.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.700
You can actually drive
that process as well.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.440
Oftentimes many people are
waiting to be recognized.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.280
You're waiting for that annual review.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.100
You're waiting for your boss or somebody

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.990
to recognize your work or the
good things that you're doing.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.450
And so the biggest thing that we can do

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.210
as young people is really
take your career by the reins

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.240
and think and make a plan for yourself.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.040
You wanna communicate with
your boss and your supervisor

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.770
to make sure you're clear number one

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.240
on what you can do to advance
your position in your role.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.670
You can set your own career growth goals.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.540
And really you can drive that process

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.010
of meeting with your supervisor.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:59.380
You might not get that raise

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.770
every quarter, every six months,

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.650
but you can still have goals
and check in to make sure

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.900
that you're clear or how are you tracking?

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.580
Are you doing the right things?

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:09.910
Are you on track for the raise

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.352
or the promotion that you are seeking out?

00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:14.500
And you're getting that feedback line.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.000
That feedback loop does
not need to be once a year.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:17.980
The goals don't really work

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:19.350
if we're only checking
in on them once a year.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.610
So beyond those six month check-ins

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.810
and making sure that
I'm on the right track

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.210
what else can I do on my own
time to build my human capital?

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.043
So, a couple of things,

00:04:28.043 --> 00:04:29.790
one, I like to say focused on education.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.510
So you can sign up for online
courses covering any topic

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:33.400
that you're interested in.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.980
So for example, if you're
marketing, social media marketing,

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.270
how to use industry specific software,

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.450
ways to improve your leadership skills.

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.710
So there's ways that you can
actually round out yourself

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.650
as a manager and a leader as well.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:45.770
A lot of the times we're focusing

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.100
just on like the on the technical skills

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.700
but there's some softer
skills that we need

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.350
as managers and leaders in
advancing the growth ladder.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.370
Another one is checking
in with your employer

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.280
on if they fund or offer

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.630
any continuing education opportunities.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.770
Will they actually pay for you to go

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.070
and take any of those classes or courses

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:08.280
to advance yourself is another
one and then certifications.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.130
So depending on your
industry there's, I'm sure

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.900
a variety of designations or
specialties or certifications

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.630
that you can get and you can
seek those out on your own.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:17.870
So what if I feel stuck?

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.380
Like I'm at a dead end in my current job.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.110
You're feeling stuck in your
current position at work.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.510
One of the things that I usually say

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.570
is go back and take stock of like,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:27.600
what are your career growth goals?

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.690
Are you in an industry that you enjoy?

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.500
Is it something that you
want to continue to pursue?

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.880
And I think the biggest thing

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.170
is don't be afraid to to jump.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.030
If you wanna go in for one last push,

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:42.350
I like to say build a
career reverse buckets.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.070
What are all of the things
that you've accomplished

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.520
in your job that you're currently in?

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.240
If you wanna go in for one last push

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.880
or there were kind of like conversation

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.100
around negotiating or growth.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.150
And if it doesn't work
out, look for another job.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.380
If you're feeling stuck,
nobody says you have to stay.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:00.240
Thanks so much Mary Beth for stopping by.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.680
So hypothetically I get
to the point in my career

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.900
where I can start investing
some actual financial capital

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.510
but how should I invest that
based on the type of career

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:09.770
that I have?

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:11.990
William Bernstein Bill, thank you so much

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:13.850
for joining us on Good Money.

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:16.570
Could you just really
quickly introduce yourself

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:18.550
and say what it is that you do?

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.360
I'm a recovering neurologist.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.540
I have a practice for 15 years.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.970
I write about finance.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:30.970
I write about economic
technological history.

00:06:32.164 --> 00:06:34.340
I do have an investment advisory firm

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:38.850
that I co-founded with a
lady named Susan Sharon,

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.663
Efficient Frontier Advisors.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.400
And that's in a nutshell, I guess.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.070
Let me ask you, based on their careers,

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:49.120
what should people be thinking

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.460
about how they should invest their money?

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:56.060
You have to ask yourself,
is your job a bond,

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.900
or is it a stock or a lottery ticket?

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.000
How are you risky as your human capital?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.600
And so if your human
capital is not very risky,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.210
if you're that tenured professor

00:07:05.751 --> 00:07:07.790
or a government employee, you can afford

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:09.640
to take lots of stock market risk.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.060
But if you're working in the
financial services industry,

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:14.580
you're already invested, your job

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.530
has already invested in the stock market.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.220
So maybe you wanna take a lot less risk

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.850
in your investment portfolio.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.150
If you look at your total capital, okay,

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:26.390
your investment capital
plus your human capital

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.980
when you're very young

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:36.440
your human capital overwhelms
your investment capital.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.170
So, there's nothing wrong
with taking a lot of risks

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.200
particularly if you have
a relatively secure job.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.000
So if you have a job that's a bond,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.430
you have a nice secure teaching position

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.940
or government position or
a reasonably secure job

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.900
in your industry with
good employment prospects

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.240
no matter where you are, then
essentially you have a lot

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.140
of bombed capital on the human side.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.270
So there's no reason why
you can't go whole hog

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:03.540
and invest all of your investment capital

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.440
when you're young in stocks.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.500
And at least the theory says,
that's what you should do.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.830
The problem with that
comes on me emotional side,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.490
which is there are very few people,

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.640
see are very few sentient
beings in this quadrant

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:19.350
of the galaxy that can
tolerate an all stock portfolio

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.040
when they're 25 years old.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.473
Let's say that you have a
more freewheeling career,

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.140
what kind of safe things should you

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:28.230
be putting your money in?

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:32.333
The classic riskless asset,
right, is of course a T bill.

00:08:32.333 --> 00:08:34.790
It's perfectly reasonably safe.

00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:38.640
The government can always
print money to pay you back.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.727
There's no credit risk
or almost no credit risk.

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:46.157
And there's almost no
duration risk either.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.460
In terms of the last few decades

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:54.460
what has changed about
what jobs are bonds stocks

00:08:56.231 --> 00:08:58.900
and has that gotten worse
or better, I suppose?

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.020
Oh, much worse.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.503
You know, we're living in
libertarian heaven right now

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:07.690
where no one has...

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.560
You know fewer and fewer
people have job security.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.630
I mean, you're a journalist,

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.430
I don't have to tell you about that.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.620
So there are far fewer
bond-like jobs out there

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:17.770
and a whole lot of jobs

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:20.443
that are more like really
bad lottery tickets.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.280
Yeah. So maybe my career is
more of a lottery ticket.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:26.333
I hope it hits.

